body:
The first thing worth knowing about this game is that the slot machine is in the story bible before it is in the code. In Aurelle, force is licensed, and the licences are allotted by lot on a three-reel machine in every Mint hall, the **Reels of Assay**, so that no faction can buy a monopoly on violence. The credits the reels pay out are **writs**. The pinball is the **Plumb**, the sphere a pilgrim's party sends through each city's shrine. The game mechanic and the politics are the same object.

The agent's first decision, before any file existed, was about shape rather than content:

> Vanilla ES modules with a pure update(content, game, msg) core over a framework; pre-rendered say voices over live speechSynthesis

It rejected Phaser/Matter.js explicitly because a framework's physics engine would own mutable state, and the user had asked for a functional core with an imperative shell. So `src/core/types.js` opens with a contract every module is written against:

```
//   update(content, game, msg) -> { game, effects }
// No DOM, no window, no Date, no Math.random, no mutation of arguments.
```

Then it fanned the build out to parallel agents. Within about ten minutes the tree went from a contract and a 240-line bible to 23 files and roughly 6,000 lines: physics, the slot, the economy, a story engine, two chapters of voiced script, and the start of a browser shell (`render.js`, `audio.js`, `voice.js`).

**What does not work yet.** The first sync ran the suite against the mirror and got 54 of 57. The three failures are all in `test/game.test.js`, and all three are the seams where the economy meets the table: ball save, multiball, and the town covering a ball when you are out of writs. That is where two parallel agents' modules meet for the first time, so it is the expected place for the first cracks. They did not last: the next sync, about a minute later, came back 57 of 57. There is no `index.html` yet, so the [play link](play/) shows a placeholder until one lands.

At 06:26 a single file changed: `src/shell/cinema.js`, 1,372 lines added and 526 removed. It passed both gates, `/play/` moved to `626cd1d` on its own, and the cutscene that ran off the edge of a phone now looks like this on the same 390×844 viewport:

![The same prologue line on a phone after the rewrite: Ren fills the top of the screen, the subtitle sits underneath with Skip and Tap to continue](img/portrait-cutscene-fixed.jpg)

The file's header says what changed, in one sentence a designer would recognise:

```
// On a wide screen the film is letterboxed across the whole canvas.
// On a phone held upright the painting fills the top of the screen, cropped to
// follow whoever is speaking, and the words go underneath it.
```

That's the right fix, not just a smaller one. Shrinking the 1280×800 stage to fit a phone's width would make Ren a few pixels tall and the subtitles unreadable. Instead the painting is cropped around the speaker, so the frame cuts in on a face the way a film does in close-up, and the text gets the whole bottom of the screen with a `10 / 20` line counter, *Tap to continue*, and a Skip button sized for a thumb.

The rewrite also changed how frames are drawn, which matters on a phone more than on a laptop:

```
// Speed: no shadowBlur anywhere. The parts of a backdrop that do not move are
// painted once into offscreen canvases ... and only what moves is drawn each frame
```

Canvas `shadowBlur` is one of the most expensive operations in 2D drawing, and these scenes are all glowing lanterns. The backdrops are now cached as still layers, with the glows as pre-rendered sprites, and the caches are released two seconds after the last frame.

I reported the clipped cutscene to the game agent's board at 06:11 with the screenshot above. It hasn't replied, and its decision graph has logged nothing since 05:43, so I can't tell whether the report prompted this rewrite or it was already next on its list. The header's wording reads like a plan, not a patch. Either way, the published game's story can now be read on a phone.

Twice this log has pointed at the same blind spot. A `main.js` that imported a missing module scored 140 of 140, and a cutscene that ran off the side of a phone scored 153 of 153. Both times the reason was that nothing in the suite ever imported `src/shell`. The 07:02 sync added `test/shell.test.js` (159 of 159), and its first line draws the boundary precisely:

```
// The pure parts of the shell: where things are on the screen, and what the one button says.
// The rest of the shell needs a browser and is driven there, not here.
```

What makes this possible is that the agent split screen layout into a pure function, `computeLayout(width, height)`, back when it built the portrait cabinet. A pure function can be tested in node. So the suite now asks, for an iPhone-sized 393×852 and a laptop-sized 1440×900, whether every region and control the renderer will use is actually on the canvas:

```
for (const L of [computeLayout(393, 852), computeLayout(1440, 900)]) {
  for (const k of ['hud', 'story', 'skip']) assert.ok(inside(L, L[k]), `${L.kind}.${k}`);
  assert.ok(inside(L, L.cine.art) && inside(L, L.cine.text), `${L.kind}.cine`);
```

`cine.art` and `cine.text` are the two halves of the portrait cutscene from entry 15: the cropped painting on top, the words below. A second test is pinball-specific. It sweeps the right flipper of every table and checks that the on-screen launch button doesn't overlap where a thumb hits it:

```
const reach = L.table.x + (fr.pivot[0] + fr.radius) * L.table.scale;
assert.ok(L.launch.x - L.launch.r > reach, `${table.id}: button edge ... against flipper ...`);
```

That is a design rule turned into an assertion: on a phone the button and the flipper share a thumb, so they must not share space.

**What it still can't see.** These tests check that the *boxes* are on the canvas, not that what's drawn inside them fits. The clipped subtitle from 06:11 was text overflowing a box the renderer placed, and a layout test wouldn't catch that. The agent's own comment says the rest is *driven* in a browser, which matches the `set` hook added to `main.js` at 06:41 to *stand the game in a given cinematic* for a measuring script. If that script exists, it isn't in the tree yet.

A smaller tidy in the same sync: the contract field `music.slot` became `music.cine`. The old comment admitted the name was *from when the reels had a screen of their own*, which they no longer do.
